---
description: Frontend SPA rules for feature-first React code
globs: frontend/src/**/*.{ts,tsx}
alwaysApply: false
---

# Frontend SPA Rules

- Keep the feature-first split intact:
  - `app/`: composition root and providers
  - `routes/`: route metadata and helpers
  - `layouts/`: shells
  - `features/`: feature UI, model/hooks, API, realtime, and feature-local helpers
  - `shared/`: cross-feature primitives only
- Within a non-trivial feature, prefer explicit `ui/`, `model/`, `api/`, `realtime/`, `theme/`, and `lib/` folders as applicable. Keep the feature root for page entrypoints and its public `index.ts`; do not create empty folders or move tiny code only to satisfy a template.
- Keep app composition in `app/`. Panel route metadata/source of truth lives in `panel-route-metadata.ts`, while `panel-route-config.tsx` adds lazy pages and optional realtime sync on top. Route trees (`app-route-tree.tsx`) and `AppRoutes` consume `panelRouteConfig`; `routes/app-routes.ts` re-exports metadata and access helpers only.
- Keep pages and layouts thin. Data loading, orchestration, permissions, route access rules, and non-trivial state transitions belong in hooks, route helpers, or feature modules.
- Decompose oversized screens and hooks by concern instead of letting one file accumulate everything. Split a large page or layout into feature-local section components (`features/<feature>/ui/`, layout siblings) and a large orchestration hook into focused hooks per concern (for game setup: draft state, save/conflict, cell media) wired by a thin composition root. Keep self-contained concerns such as the questions catalog inside their owning section. Do not add abstractions for one-off, single-use components.
- Keep feature boundaries explicit. When a feature is consumed by another feature, expose a public API (`features/<feature>/index.ts`) instead of deep imports into a neighbor's `api/`, `model/`, or `ui/` internals.
- Use the shared `openapi-fetch` clients over generated `paths`. Keep endpoint templates static and pass path/query/body through typed options; do not add raw `fetch`, dynamic endpoint strings, manual response generics, or local transport shapes.
- Keep one feature API/data-access layer by default. Split out a domain adapter only when it adds behavior such as `404 -> null`, transport-to-domain mapping, multipart adaptation, or optimistic cache updates; do not create pass-through `api -> data-access` modules.
- Keep feature-specific transports, realtime event handlers, resync logic, and mutation orchestration inside the owning feature. Shared realtime code may own connection lifecycle only; it must not know feature event semantics.
- Use TanStack Query as the owner of server state. Do not mirror query results into Zustand, context, or another global store. Keep query keys and `queryOptions` inside the owning feature, use `mutationOptions` for repeated invalidation/error policies, and update cached data through Query invalidation or `setQueryData`.
- Prefer focused mutation hooks over aggregate hooks that initialize unrelated operations for every consumer.
- Treat generated OpenAPI types as the compile-time transport source of truth. Add Zod runtime parsing selectively at critical or externally unstable response boundaries; keep schemas adjacent to the owning boundary and type-check them against generated aliases instead of creating a second hand-maintained API model.
- Keep navigation and access control declarative. Route definitions, redirects, default panel routes, and role-aware UI visibility should come from shared route/auth helpers, not duplicated inline checks.
- Keep route-level access and capability-level access separated: route visibility belongs to route helpers, while action-level checks belong to shared panel capability helpers.
- Treat frontend role gating as UX only; backend remains authoritative.
- Put all user-facing text in feature-owned `i18n/*-translations.ts` modules. Compose resources in `src/locales/index.ts`, keep i18next type augmentation enabled, and do not bypass typed keys with broad `string` casts.
- Use React Hook Form with `zodResolver` for transactional submitted forms such as game creation, invitations, and registration settings when those flows exist. Keep schemas outside components in the owning feature, derive values with `z.infer`, and use shared controlled form primitives. Dense interactive editors such as game-board draft cells stay controlled React state rather than becoming one giant RHF form.
- Add Zod runtime parsing selectively at critical or externally unstable API response boundaries. Generated OpenAPI types remain the compile-time transport source of truth.
- Keep component-local UI state local. Introduce Zustand only for genuinely cross-tree client-only state with multiple consumers; never use it as a default modal container, auth-session owner, or server-state mirror.
- Keep feature-specific code out of `shared/`.
- Keep UI reuse layered inside `shared/ui`:
  - primitives (controls and base building blocks),
  - patterns (page/section/layout composition),
  - feedback (dialog/toast/loading/error/empty states).
- Keep `shared/ui` physically structured by those layers (`shared/ui/primitives`, `shared/ui/patterns`, `shared/ui/feedback`) and expose them through barrel exports instead of ad-hoc file-level imports.
- Prefer `shared/ui/index.ts` barrel imports for shared UI usage in features.
- Prefer shared UI contracts over raw MUI duplication:
  - actions via `AppButton`/`AppLinkButton`,
  - submitted fields via shared React Hook Form adapters,
  - repeated async section states via shared async/state components,
  - repeated toast/dialog wiring via shared feedback components.
- Keep visual tokens centralized in `hunt-palette.ts`, `tokens.ts`, and `surface-sx.ts`. Compose the MUI theme from `app/theme/palette.ts`, `typography.ts`, and `component-overrides.ts` through `appTheme.ts`. Repeated cross-feature styles belong in theme/shared UI; feature-specific presets belong in `features/<feature>/theme/`.
- MUI + Emotion is the styling system. Keep the existing MUI feedback layer unless a replacement solves a concrete product need; do not add a second global toast system.
- Add icon, animation, SVG-component, or brand-icon dependencies only with the feature that uses them. Prefer Lucide for general-purpose icons and Framer Motion for complex presence/layout transitions when those needs appear, but do not install or wrap them preemptively.
- For row/column board-like layouts, use shared matrix/grid primitives before adding another feature-local grid shell.
- Keep speculative roadmap UI out of production pages. Future features belong in docs/issues until they have a real user flow; unavailable states should show concise user-facing feedback without disabled mock controls.
- Prefer env/config switches over rewriting feature code when transport or runtime behavior changes. Keep mock-friendly seams, but treat real HTTP flow as the target path.
- Use Vitest for unit tests and React Testing Library for hooks/components. Prioritize setup draft/save/conflict, registration mutation policies, realtime models, route/capability access, validation, cache behavior, and non-trivial feature model transitions. Add page-level tests for key loading, error, empty, unavailable, and success states; prefer focused behavioral coverage over broad snapshot-style tests.
- Keep Playwright as a small smoke layer rather than duplicating component coverage. Add 3–5 stable user-flow scenarios only after deterministic test data and auth fixtures exist.
